course: dictionary
lesson: 8937
title: Sikhism: a faith from the Punjab
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Sikhism is a religion. It was founded in the Punjab region of India by Guru Nanak about 500 years ago. The word «Sikh» names a person who follows Sikhism.
explain: Sikhs believe in one God. They follow the teachings of the ten Gurus and their holy book, the Guru Granth Sahib.
explain: Sikhism teaches that all people are equal, whatever their family, wealth or religion. You can see how this idea shapes daily life in the gurdwara, the Sikh temple.
fact: Sikhism is a religion founded in the Punjab region of India by Guru Nanak.
fact: Sikhs worship in a gurdwara, which is a Sikh temple.
fact: At the gurdwara, a free kitchen called the langar feeds everyone.
fact: Many Sikhs wear the five Ks, such as the kara (steel bracelet) and kesh (uncut hair), and men often wear a turban.
fact: Sikhs live in many countries, including the UK, Canada, the USA and Australia.
exercise: Where was Sikhism founded? | Rome in Italy | *The Punjab region of India | Egypt | Scotland
exercise: Who founded Sikhism? | Muhammad | *Guru Nanak | Jesus | Buddha
exercise: What is a gurdwara? | A shop selling bracelets | A kind of turban | *A Sikh temple where people worship | A school for the Gurus
test: What is the langar? | The steel bracelet worn by Sikhs | *A free kitchen that feeds everyone at the gurdwara | A prayer said at dawn | Holy book read at the gurdwara
test: What do Sikhs believe about God? | That God lives only in the Punjab | That God does not exist | *That there is one God | That there are many gods, one for each Guru
exam: Which holy book do Sikhs follow? | Bible | Quran | Torah | *Guru Granth Sahib
